<lane ...input id="ce" name="Compound Engineering"
  by="Every (Kieran Klaassen, Trevin Chow) · Claude Code, Cursor, Codex and 11 more" source=ce>
  <@summary>
    One plan file grows from requirements to units; review traces back to its IDs. The Compound step writes down what the next change should not have to rediscover. Order shown is the one the <skill-ref of=ce name="lfg" path="skills/lfg/SKILL.md"/> command runs; there Brainstorm is an optional route and Compound runs only when the work corrected a wrong belief.
  </@summary>
  <@order label="Order, per the author" lead="recommended in docs; the lists disagree">
    README: "The core loop is six steps: brainstorm the requirements, plan the implementation, work through the plan, simplify what you wrote, review the result, then compound the learning". The older guide on every.to has four: "Plan → Work → Review → Compound → Repeat". <file-ref of=ce name="docs/guides/README.md"/> chains brainstorm, plan, work, compound and calls review and simplify on-demand. Ship appears only in <skill-ref of=ce name="lfg" path="skills/lfg/SKILL.md"/>, which runs Compound before the PR; the <skill-ref of=ce name="ce-work" path="skills/ce-work/SKILL.md"/> guide puts it after shipping. Typed by hand, nothing enforces any of these orders.
  </@order>
  <@owner from="brainstorm" to="brainstorm" name="ce-brainstorm" note="skill"/>
  <@owner from="plan" to="plan" name="ce-plan" note="skill; ends by invoking ce-doc-review"/>
  <@owner from="work" to="work" name="ce-work" note="skill"/>
